  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 210 AM CDT Sat Aug 15 2026\n\nShowers continue to track east across central ND early this\nmorning. Surface low pressure was situated around the Turtle\nMountains with a southerly surface flow into the James River\nValley. Back to the west, a northerly surface flow was bringing\nlow stratus into western ND.\n\nFor today, showers are expected to exit the Turtle Mountains\narea into the James River Valley by mid morning, if not sooner.\nThe majority of the day is expected to be rain free. However a\nsprinkle or brief shower can not be ruled out in the far west\nthis afternoon. Look for highs mostly in the 70s today with some\nlower 80s possible in the southern James River Valley. Expect \ncloudy skies this morning, with patchy fog, especially north. \nSkies are expected to slowly clear from west to east this \nafternoon. The north central into the northern James River \nValley may not see much of the sun until late afternoon. It will\nalso be breezy late this morning and this afternoon, especially\nover central ND with northwest winds of 15 to 20 mph and some \ngusts to around 30 mph.\n\nSkies will clear tonight as high pressure builds over the state.\nLow temperatures Sunday morning will range from the mid 40s to\nmid 50s.\n\nA northwest upper level flow become established on Sunday. As \nsurface high pressure tracks east during the day, we see return \nflow into western ND Sunday afternoon. The atmosphere becomes \nslightly unstable in the southwest Sunday afternoon and \nshortwave energy begins to impede upon the state as it works its\nway through a mean western upper ridge. Bulk shear is initially\nmodest Sunday but increases Sunday afternoon to 30 to 40 knots \nor so, It's possible we could see an afternoon thunderstorm in \nthe far southwest Sunday with possibly some small hail or \nlocally gusty winds. Currently the SPC has a marginal risk for\nsevere storms (level 2 out of 5) just to the south, in western \nSouth Dakota. The CSU machine learning model does bring a low \nrisk for severe hail a bit farther north, into southwest ND. \nLater Sunday night another shortwave tracks southeast across the\nforecast area, bringing a chance of showers and isolated \nthunderstorms to the area. Shear will remain moderate to strong \nbut instability is modest at best. Current thinking is that the \nthreat for stronger storms will be low.\n\nWe see a brief warm-up Monday, especially in the southwest where\nhigh could reach the mid 80s. A northern Stream shortwave and\nattendant cold front, rotating around a Hudson Bay low will\nbring a cold front through the area with a chance of showers \nand Thunderstorms Monday into Tuesday, with cooler temperatures \nTuesday and Wednesday. A northwest upper flow remains over the\narea through the rest of the work week. After Wednesday, the \nHudson Bay low moves off to the east and a western upper level \nridge become better established just to our west. This should \nbring a warming trend as we go through the end of the work week \nand into next weekend. With a northwest upper flow continuing, a\nlow threat for showers and thunderstorms will remain in the \nforecast.",
